Exercise 2.2 

A good test for this portion should:

- Control the current time so the test is deterministic.
- Keep priority, status, tags, and `updatedAt` identical.
- Choose dates that clearly fall into distinct categories.
- Assert the expected score difference, not only that one score is larger.

Your pseudocode compares two tasks, but a future task might receive a bonus too. That could hide an incorrect overdue bonus.

### One stronger example

```javascript
test('adds the overdue bonus to an otherwise identical task', () => {
  // Freeze time so date calculations do not depend on when the test runs.
  jest.useFakeTimers();
  jest.setSystemTime(new Date('2026-09-08T12:00:00Z'));

  // Old enough to avoid the recently-updated bonus.
  const updatedAt = new Date('2026-09-01T12:00:00Z');

  const overdueTask = {
    priority: TaskPriority.MEDIUM,
    dueDate: new Date('2026-09-07T12:00:00Z'),
    status: null,
    tags: [],
    updatedAt
  };

  const taskWithNoDueDateBonus = {
    priority: TaskPriority.MEDIUM,
    dueDate: new Date('2026-09-20T12:00:00Z'),
    status: null,
    tags: [],
    updatedAt
  };

  const overdueScore = calculateTaskScore(overdueTask);
  const futureScore = calculateTaskScore(taskWithNoDueDateBonus);

  // Both tasks have a base score of 20.
  // The overdue task receives 30 additional points.
  expect(overdueScore).toBe(50);
  expect(futureScore).toBe(20);
  expect(overdueScore - futureScore).toBe(30);

  jest.useRealTimers();
});
```

The exact values here are based on the current scoring policy: medium priority contributes $20$, and an overdue date contributes $30$.

